The page continues a discussion of 'recollements' (gluings) in (Cat), the category of small categories.

Setting. A category X is decomposed as U ⊂ X ⊃ Y, where:
- U and Y are complementary full subcategories;
- there are no arrows from Y to U, so U is a cosieve-type 'open' piece and Y a sieve-type 'closed' piece, or the reverse depending on convention.

Such an X is completely determined by U, Y and the bifunctor H_X : U^op × Y → Ens, H_X(u,y) = Hom_X(u,y). In modern language X is the collage (cograph) of the profunctor H_X. Equivalently, X is a category with a functor X → Δ^1 whose fibres are U and Y.

Grothendieck then describes the basic operations in terms of these data.

(a) Functors out of a decomposed category. A functor f : X → Z amounts to:
- f_U : U → Z;
- f_Y : Y → Z;
- a morphism of bifunctors H_X(u,y) → Hom_Z(f_U u, f_Y y).
This is the universal property of the collage.

(b) Morphisms of decomposed categories. A functor f : X' → X is compatible with given decompositions X' = U' ∪ Y' and X = U ∪ Y when U' = f^{-1}(U) and Y' = f^{-1}(Y). It amounts to:
- f_{U'} : U' → U;
- f_{Y'} : Y' → Y;
- a bifunctorial map H_{X'}(u',y') → H_X(f u', f y').

(c) Products. For any category T, T×U ⊂ T×X ⊃ T×Y is again a decomposition, with H_{T×X}((t,u),(t',y)) ≅ Hom_T(t,t') × H_X(u,y).

(d) Homotopies. A homotopy h : Δ^1 × X' → X (the symbol is written as a bold I/Δ^1) is compatible with the decompositions when h^{-1}(U) = Δ^1 × U' and h^{-1}(Y) = Δ^1 × Y'. Equivalently, both ends h_0 and h_1 induce the same decomposition U', Y'. Combining (b) and (c), such an h is given by:
- h_{U'} : Δ^1 × U' → U;
- h_{Y'} : Δ^1 × Y' → Y;
- a map, bifunctorial in (s,u') and (t,y'), Hom(s,t) × H_{X'}(u',y') → H_X(h_{U'}(s,u'), h_{Y'}(t,y')).

Purpose. This sets up the category of 'recollement data' (U, Y, H) and its homotopy theory. It presumably serves to show that the homotopy type of X is determined, in Hot, by U, Y and H, e.g. as a homotopy pushout of U ← ∫H → Y. That is the kind of input needed for the 'cohomology properties of maps in (Cat)' and the sums/products applications announced on the previous page.
